you  we know that people who fit in enjoy  their work they enjoy the experience of  being in an organization they're  emotionally rewarded by it and we all  aspire I I imagine to create  organizations where members of the  organization wake up in the morning and  want to come and where they feel  attached and indebted to the  organization and mostly indebted to  their peers and and and by creating a  healthy cultural environment we can  facilitate that  it's the experience of coming to an  entirely different cultural context that  makes visible to you how pervasive  culture is but also how invisible it is  so like an example I always like to use  is when I came to the States for the  first time and people would say to me  let's have coffee sometime and I would  take that as a literal invitation to  having coffee sometime and I didn't  quite understand that it was in fact  like a very nice pleasant way to finish  a conversation but where the intention  is actually I don't want to have coffee  with you  but let's just pretend that we had a  nice interaction and that so I would I  would initially respond by saying oh  yeah sure let's have coffee sometime  when it took me a while to figure out  that that wasn't the intention of the  speaker and it's only when you're kind  of transposed to very different cultural  settings that suddenly your lack of  cultural skill becomes apparent to you  and that moment to me and have  highlighted how important but invisible  it is and why it's important for us to  study it  culture is about the systematic  distribution of of beliefs and and  cognitive understandings across a  population and it's very difficult to  measure people's cognition historically  we've been doing this by asking them  questions through surveys the problem is  that surveys are not very good  instruments because we lie to ourselves  we lie to others we sometimes are not  very well attached to our cognition and  especially our unselfconscious cognition  that's what makes it unselfconscious so  and add to that the fact that it's not  very feasible to ask people questions  every day time and again it it leads to  huge attrition and disengagement  you  the internet revolution has made a  variety of behaviors that up until  recently were not recorded recordable  and they leave a trace from how we  communicate online to our purchasing  behaviors everything now leaves a trace  and marry that with the computational  capabilities that we now have our  ability just our ability to compute on  our on our smart phones we now have  computational capabilities that are akin  to what NASA had in 1985 and that was  new machine learning tools artificial  intelligence deep learning the ability  to analyze unstructured complex data and  to reduce it to measurable outcomes both  of these together bring us to the golden  age of cultural analysis  so we were very lucky to have  collaborated with a few organizations  already that we're willing to share  information deriving from the email  exchange amongst employees over a period  of of multiple years by now myself my  colleagues and all the members of our  our lab we've analyzed many many years  of data being exchanged by members of  multiple organizations and there's a lot  of information culturally that we can  derive by looking at how people use  language in their emails  the reason that language is such an  useful way for us to analyze culture is  because language is probably the most  important medium through which cultural  information is is exchanged between  between individuals I'm not interested  in whether when you and I are talking  are we talking about how are we going to  address this particular customer problem  or are we gonna talk about HR issues or  gonna talk about the Christmas party  that's coming up  what functionally were exchanging is  immaterial to the analysis what's  interesting is whether in discussing  these topics were using the same kind of  linguistic styles as a reflection of our  cultural alignment so whether we're  using my aggressive and brief language  or whether we're very apologetic and you  can just think about all your email  exchanges there's a significant  difference between saying dear so-and-so  it was such a pleasure to meet you and I  hope that this is not an intrusion on  your time etc but if you don't mind that  your earliest convenience  can we please meet and you can say the  exact same thing but say hey John can we  meet next week without all of these like  introductions and apologies functionally  you've exchanged the exact same  information but there's a lot of  cultural content embedded in how you  chose to present yourself and and and  the problem and the question and that's  precisely what what our analysis tries  to to infer  you  swear words are very from a symbolic  point of view they're very aggressive  somebody drops an f-bomb  it's noticeable it's not it's not it's  not a subtle thing that people do as I  speak and if you think about the  organizations that you've been in or  their various cultural context in which  you've gone through through your life  there's a lot of variation in terms of  whether it's allowed or really a faux  pas to use swear words in an interaction  and you can imagine that there are  certain organizations where not only do  people swear a lot they use swearing as  a means to project to others their  authority and their tenacity and you can  imagine at the other extreme that there  are organizations where the use of swear  words is perceived as an offensive and  inappropriate way of handling yourself  would be interpreted as aggression so  it's the same use of language but it's  interpreted very differently as a  function of what's normatively  appropriate or inappropriate in these in  these two different settings  in the research that I do and where we  analyze how people use language in their  interactions their habitual way of  speaking inside the organization whether  they swear at the appropriate level is a  very strong signal of the extent to  which they're aligned or misaligned with  the normative expectations of their  peers the mere information about whether  or not an individual is correctly using  language insofar as how language is  being used by others and reflecting  their expectations and beliefs about the  world is a very strong indicator of  whether that person reads the cultural  code and whether conditional having read  it correctly whether that person has the  motivation to behave in a culturally  compliant way  you can imagine that some people realize  that it's inappropriate to swear but  they swear nevertheless and that  decision whether conscious or on self  conscious has a lot of information there  either signaling to others that they  don't want to be part of this group or  they might feel so secure in their  position in the group that they believed  that by behaving in a non conformist way  they can signal to others their  uniqueness without undermining their  position in the organization so there's  a lot of information in these very  subtle and automatic decisions that we  make and how we communicate information  to one another  discoveries on culture are both exciting  and daunting they're exciting because  we're starting to figure out the  constituent components of this opaque  thing that we call culture they're  daunting because we discover how complex  it is how difficult it is to manage and  how not one size fit all or one shot  exercise it is but again they're  exciting because I think were at this  moment where the research can be  translated into insights and into  methodologies and into tools that can  inform managerial decisions in a way  that would make management more  effective and more rewarding to  everybody involved  [Music]

